# set = new Set; set.add(member); for (const each of set) each()        for (;;) { step = next(); value = step.value; if (step.done) break; value() }
# The step that finds the end answers undefined (primitive 0) as its value. A for..of reads the value behind the branch on `done`, where the step has not found the end: what it reads is what the set holds.
# A value that is read in front of the branch may be that of the last step.
